ake Away", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"type": "post_instructions", "contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_skills", "contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}]}], "id": 10, "type": "DWS"}, {"questions": [{"responses": [{"_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "selected": 0, "id": 0, "correct": 0}, {"_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "selected": 0, "id": 1, "correct": 1}], "_data": [{"type": "question", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}]}], "id": 0}], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": []}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Activity 3", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}], "id": 11, "type": "EXT"}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n6le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "100", "text": "<strong>Megan:</strong>  Wow, there are countless data types in PHP!</br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong> Megan, you are overreacting! There are only eight; String, Integer, Float, Boolean, Array, Object, NULL and\u2026 I always forget the last one.</br>\n<strong>Megan:</strong> Resource, right? </br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong> That one! Thanks! I've never used it because I understand it is an advanced type.</br>\n<strong>Megan:</strong>  At first sight, no one would suppose PHP needs considerable expertise since beginners have been using it for a while now. Anyways, Leo, help me review them all!</br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong> Sure, let's start with string.What do you know about it?</br>\n<strong>Megan:</strong>  String is a sequence of characters, like \u201cthis is the best\u201d, right?</br></br>", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "50000", "text": "<strong>Leo:</strong> That's basically it. What about integer?</br>\n<strong>Megan:</strong>  Integer is a non-decimal number between -2,147,483,648 and its positive form. It has to have at least 1 digit, but it should never have a decimal point.</br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong> Wow, I wouldn't have thought you were so good at remembering numbers until now!</br>\n<strong>Megan:</strong>  Hey, that's rude! For your information, I'm pretty good at numbers.</br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong> I'm sorry. Let's continue. What do you know about Boolean or Array?</br>\n<strong>Megan:</strong> Nothing!</br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong> Okay. Boolean represents two possible states, true or false, so it's very suitable for conditional testing. On the other hand, Array is useful to store multiple values in one variable.</br></br>", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob05re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "104500", "text": "<strong>Megan:</strong>  Well, I thought they would be more difficult. What about NULL? NULL has appeared automatically many times when using PHP. </br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong>  That's because NULL doesn't have a value, and if you create a variable with no value, it is automatically assigned as a NULL type.</br>\n<strong>Megan:</strong>  Oh, that explains it. There is only one left; Object, what do we use that for?</br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong> It stores data and information on how to process that data. As a rule, it must always be explicitly declared, or it won't work.</br>\n<strong>Megan:</strong>  Thanks, Leo. You have been of great help.</br>\n<strong>Leo:</strong> That's only because I have been working here for a while.", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob05re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le02ob05re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}